    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Ford Rolls Out the Ranger MS-RT Champion Edition: You Can Choose Any Color As Long as It Is Black\nHarley-Davidson Black Reaper Is Here to Snuff Out the Shine of the Low Rider ST\n54 photos\nPhoto: Thunderbike\nPeople’s taste in motorcycles is just as varied as people themselves, but if you really think about it, riders are part of two major categories: they either like their bikes chrome and shiny, or monochrome and, if possible, matte. This here story is (kind of) for those in the latter category.\nBike makers, including Harley-Davidson, know this, and that’s why most of the time they try their best to satisfy both tastes. They can’t help but show what kind of bodywork they prefer, though, and that’s why they generally lean to promote in a more obvious fashion one or the other.\nTake the present-day Low Rider ST , which is offered in two trim levels, chrome and black, with four associated colors. No matter the choice, the Low Rider shows shiny elements in various quantities.\nAll the shine Harley includes on this bike from the factory floor has been completely snuffed out on the Black Reaper custom Low Rider ST we have here. Look all you want, and, with the exception of the fork legs, you won’t see a single trace of shiny chrome or any other metal.\nThe all-black club-styler, as German shop Thunderbike, its maker, calls it, is meant to send across a message of performance and unforgiveness. And if you ask me, it manages to do just that, even if, technically speaking, the Softail frame and Milwaukee-Eight engine are the same as they were when the bike rolled off the lines.\nThe bagger has been at the receiving end of the usual customization choices, starting with the fitting of new wheels and Brembo braking hardware and ending with the Dr. Jekill & Mr. Hyde silencers pulled over the exhaust system.\nHaving also modified the bike’s suspension system, Thunderbike was careful enough to make the motorcycle black in its entirety, and to gift it with only the tiniest touches of red for contrast and a more impressive appearance.\nEverywhere you look, the changes made jump out to take you by surprise: the red brake caliper at the rear from under the swingarm, the original handlebars from where they have been relocated, and the Saddlemen step-up seat from high up on the frame.\nAt the front, the two-wheeler displays a Klock Werks windscreen, satin grips, and aftermarket turn signals, which at the front are hidden under the distinctive ST fairing. An impressive touch is the removal of the factory footpegs and the fitting of mini footboards in their place. This choice was made as a means to make long rides as comfortable as possible for the rider.\nNot including the mechanical upgrades, such as they are, and the paint job, Thunderbike used some 20 custom parts from its inventory. Combined, they amount to $5,600, and that should give you a pretty decent idea of how much the project is worth.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nThunderbike Harley-Davidson low rider st Harley-Davidson harley-davidson black reaper\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT 1969 Ford Cobra Has the Great Original Ram Air 428 and Low Price, No One Cares It's a 1-of-1",
